<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  App target. Belongs at the root of the App bundle, which is what adding it to the
  target's Copy Bundle Resources phase does. docs/IOS.md step 4 wires it up.

  Read Apple's reason texts before touching the codes below: several codes share a
  category and they are not interchangeable, and the obvious-looking one is often
  about *displaying* the value rather than using it.

  FileTimestamp
    C617.1 — metadata of files inside the app container, the app group container or
      the CloudKit container. That is MeteoRideShareStore, which reads
      `.modificationDate` in two places, and the reason names all three containers
      because two of them are used: `prune` drops anything the web layer never collected
      in 24 hours from the folder in the App Group container, and `recoverSystemInbox`
      applies the same 24-hour rule to the copies iOS leaves in Documents/Inbox, which is
      the app container. It is also the reason
      @capacitor/filesystem's README asks for, and for the same kind of access.
      Not DDA9.1, which is for *displaying* a timestamp to the person — nothing here
      shows one. Not 3B52.1 either, which is for files the person specifically granted
      access to through something like a document picker; the inbox is the app's own.

  UserDefaults
    CA92.1 — user defaults readable only by this app. @capacitor/preferences writes
      `UserDefaults.standard` and its `group` option is a key prefix, not a suite name
      (node_modules/@capacitor/preferences/ios/Sources/PreferencesPlugin/Preferences.swift),
      so nothing here shares defaults through the App Group. Not 1C8F.1, which is for
      defaults shared with an App Group, and emphatically not 54BD.1, which is about
      active keyboard information and has nothing to do with user defaults.

  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is deliberately absent, and its absence is not a claim
  that nothing is collected. Apple takes an app's own collection declaration through the
  App Privacy questionnaire in App Store Connect, which is where the nuance fits and
  where docs/IOS.md says what to answer. This file cannot hold it: the route's
  coordinates go to Open-Meteo unlinked, but the same coordinates go to OpenWeather
  alongside the user's own API key, which OpenWeather can tie to its own customer — so
  the data is linked on one provider's path and not on the other's, and a single
  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked boolean must be one or the other. Declaring
  everything unlinked contradicted what public/privacy-ios.html says about that key;
  declaring everything linked would overstate the default path, which is the one nearly
  everyone uses. The form, where both paths can be described, is the honest place.

  What must NOT happen either way is ticking "Data Not Collected": the route's
  coordinates leave the device on every forecast and Open-Meteo's logs may hold them for
  up to 90 days, so the ephemeral-processing exemption does not apply.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
